  "body": "Summary\n\nI found that wiTryVaultComposer sets minAmountLD equal to assets (amount sent) in both _fastRedeem() and _handleUnstake() functions. This configuration fails to account for LayerZero's mandatory dust removal mechanism, which truncates amounts to fit cross-chain decimal precision.\n\nSince iTryTokenOFT inherits the default sharedDecimals of 6, any transfer amount not perfectly divisible by 10^12 will fail with SlippageExceeded. This results in a Denial of Service (DoS) for users attempting to redeem or unstake uneven amounts (which is extremely common due to yield accrual).\n\nThis exact issue was previously identified and fixed in LayerZero's OVault audit by Cantina, where it was rated as Medium severity.\n\nVulnerability Details\nLooking at wiTryVaultComposer.sol, slippage is hardcoded to zero in two critical functions:\n\n1. _fastRedeem (Lines 116-117)\n\nSolidity\n\n_sendParam.amountLD = assets;\n_sendParam.minAmountLD = assets; // <--- Vulnerability: 0% slippage\n2. _handleUnstake (Lines 267-268)\n\nSolidity\n\namountLD: assets,\nminAmountLD: assets, // <--- Vulnerability: 0% slippage\nThe Root Cause: LayerZero Dust Removal\nThe LayerZero OFT standard forces dust removal before checking slippage. According to OFTCore.sol:\n\nSolidity\n\nfunction _debitView(uint256 _amountLD, uint256 _minAmountLD, uint32) \n    internal view virtual returns (uint256 amountSentLD, uint256 amountReceivedLD) \n{\n    // @dev Remove the dust so nothing is lost on the conversion between chains\n    amountSentLD = _removeDust(_amountLD);\n    amountReceivedLD = amountSentLD;\n\n    // @dev Check for slippage.\n    if (amountReceivedLD < _minAmountLD) {\n        revert SlippageExceeded(amountReceivedLD, _minAmountLD); // <--- Reverts here\n    }\n}\nSince iTryTokenOFT.sol uses the default shared decimals of 6 and local decimals of 18, the conversion rate is 10^12.\n\nExample: A user redeems 100.000000000000000001 tokens (1 wei of dust from yield).\n\nDust Removal: 100.0...01 becomes 100.0...00.\n\nCheck: 100.0...00 (received) < 100.0...01 (minAmount).\n\nResult: Transaction Reverts.\n\nImpact\n\nDenial of Service: Users cannot use Fast Redeem or Unstake features if their balance includes any dust (which is almost guaranteed due to yield calculations).\n\nWasted Assets: Users pay for source chain execution and LayerZero messaging fees, only for the transaction to revert on the destination chain.\n\nNo Workaround: Since the slippage is hardcoded in the contract, users cannot manually adjust it to make their transactions succeed.\n\nPrior Art\n\nThis is a known integration issue with LayerZero V2. Cantina identified this exact bug in LayerZero's own OVault implementation:\n\n\"Different slippage checks can lead to stuck funds - executeOVaultAction() has a minAmount check, but LayerZeroEndpoint::send() has a slightly different minAmount check in _debitView(). With specific amounts, it could pass the first check but fail the second.\"\n\nSource: Cantina Audit - LayerZero OVault\n\nProof of Concept\nI have created a runnable PoC that simulates the dust removal logic and demonstrates the revert.\n\nSolidity\n\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.20;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\n\ncontract DustRemovalPoC is Test {\n    uint256 constant DECIMAL_CONVERSION_RATE = 1e12;\n\n    function _removeDust(uint256 _amountLD) internal pure returns (uint256) {\n        return (_amountLD / DECIMAL_CONVERSION_RATE) * DECIMAL_CONVERSION_RATE;\n    }\n\n    function test_fastRedeem_reverts_with_dust() public {\n        // User fast redeems, vault returns amount with precision from yield\n        uint256 assets = 100e18 + 1; // 100 tokens + 1 wei\n        \n        // Contract sets minAmountLD = assets (the bug)\n        uint256 minAmountLD = assets;\n        \n        // LayerZero removes dust\n        uint256 afterDust = _removeDust(assets);\n        \n        // This is what LayerZero checks\n        bool reverts = afterDust < minAmountLD;\n        \n        console.log(\"Assets from vault:\", assets);\n        console.log(\"After dust removal:\", afterDust);\n        console.log(\"Reverts:\", reverts);\n        \n        assertTrue(reverts, \"Should revert due to dust\");\n    }\n\n    function test_failure_rate() public {\n        uint256 failures = 0;\n        // Simulate 100 transactions with random dust\n        for (uint256 i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {\n            uint256 assets = i * 1e18 + (i * 123456789);\n            if (_removeDust(assets) < assets) failures++;\n        }\n        \n        console.log(\"Failure rate:\", failures, \"/ 100\");\n        assertGt(failures, 95);\n    }\n}\n\n\nTested Output\n\nRunning the PoC confirms the vulnerability with a near 100% failure rate for granular amounts:\n\nPlaintext\n\n[PASS] test_fastRedeem_reverts_with_dust()\nLogs:\n  Assets from vault: 100000000000000000001\n  After dust removal: 100000000000000000000\n  Reverts: true\n\n[PASS] test_failure_rate()\nLogs:\n  Failure rate: 100 / 100\nRecommended Mitigation\nThe cleanest fix is to pre-apply dust removal in the Composer contract before setting minAmountLD, ensuring it matches LayerZero's calculation.\n\nAdd a helper function and use it when constructing SendParam:\n\nSolidity\n\nfunction _removeDust(uint256 _amountLD) internal view returns (uint256) {\n    uint256 rate = 10 ** (18 - IOFT(ASSET_OFT).sharedDecimals());\n    return (_amountLD / rate) * rate;\n}\nFix for _fastRedeem:\n\nDiff\n\n  _sendParam.amountLD = assets;\n- _sendParam.minAmountLD = assets;\n+ _sendParam.minAmountLD = _removeDust(assets);\nFix for _handleUnstake:\n\nDiff\n\n  amountLD: assets,\n- minAmountLD: assets,\n+ minAmountLD: _removeDust(assets),",
